--- body ---
## Context
Author request, 2026-11-01. Imagine running Cirdi as a purely internal
service: hosted on a CIRED server, at a centre-cired.fr subdomain, reachable
only from the lab (network or SSO to be decided), and drop all personal data
collection.

What exists today: a public VPS (deploy/ops/common_config.sh), a public site
and API over HTTP (ticket 0004), and a monitor service (src/monitor/) that
records client IPs, profiles and queries per event, plus analysis code
(src/analysis/) and an anonymised dataset (reports/monitor-logs-anon_*.zip)
built from those records (used for the usage analyses and the MeSSH26 talk).

## Imagine first
Design note (harness template: problem, situation from git, state of the art,
alternatives, synthesis with costs, phased plan, double advisor review +
KISS). Questions it must settle:
- Hosting: which CIRED server, who administers it, backups; fit with the
  self-hosted Albert option of ticket 0009 (GPU, latency).
- Access: lab network only, VPN, or CNRS/lab SSO; whether any public demo
  remains.
- Data: remove the monitor service and the frontend telemetry entirely, or
  keep aggregate counters with no identifiers; what this ends for the usage
  research (the anonymised dataset stays as a closed historical series).
- Effect on other tickets: 0001 (privacy notice becomes "no personal data"),
  0004 (public HTTPS and spend cap shrink to internal TLS and quotas), 0005
  (monitor hardening becomes monitor removal), 0007 (ops scripts retargeted
  to the CIRED server).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Design note accepted by the author, with the hosting, access and data choices and their costs
- [ ] Tickets 0001, 0004, 0005 and 0007 updated or closed to match the decision
- [ ] If adopted: service running on the CIRED server at its centre-cired.fr subdomain, unreachable from outside, with no personal data collected (checked by inspecting requests and server logs)
